*.hprof
.jqwik-database

# Local-only security audit (never publish — it is an attacker roadmap)
SECURITY-AUDIT.local.md

# Secrets / credentials (audit I2): defence-in-depth against an accidental `git add` of a local
# keystore, env file, or credential before the repo goes public. The project externalizes all
# secrets via ${env:VAR}, so none of these belong in version control. These are extension-anchored
# (plus a few well-known key filenames) so they never shadow legitimately-named source files — no
# broad `*secret*`/`credentials*` substring globs, which collide with sources like SecretMap.java
# under git's case-insensitive matching and would otherwise need a fragile `!*.md`-style re-include
# block (which itself un-ignored SECURITY-AUDIT.local.md above).
*.env
.env
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
*.truststore
*.kdbx
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
# Well-known credential filenames (the I2 footgun): GCP service-account keys and the like are
# commonly named credentials.json / *-credentials.json / service-account*.json. These are exact
# enough not to collide with any source file (verified: SecretMap.java and friends stay tracked).
credentials.json
*-credentials.json
service-account*.json
gcloud-*.json

# Security-scanner output dropped in the repo root by /vulnhunt runs
# (cesium-kafka_VULNHUNT_RESULTS_<timestamp>/). Local triage artefacts: they can restate findings
# verbatim, including not-yet-fixed ones, so they must never be committed.
*_VULNHUNT_RESULTS_*/
